{
  "dom_id": "step-q143016",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Compute the values of $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ for which the polar curve $r=3$ has vertical tangents, given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/ddbc211d7787b88636f4688d60ef712f56b382f74afd35ad1b8599a380dec4fb.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
